People tried for decades to build a machine that sewed. In 1830, a French tailor named Barthélemy Thimonnier patented a machine that made a simple chain of loops. The French army ordered uniforms from his workshop. Then a crowd of tailors, afraid for their jobs, broke in and smashed the machines.

In the USA, Walter Hunt built a lockstitch machine in the 1830s but never patented it. Elias Howe patented his version in 1846. A few years later Isaac Singer built a more practical machine with a needle that moved straight up and down and a foot pedal to drive it. Howe sued Singer and others for copying his ideas. In the end, the makers agreed to share their [[@patents|patents]] and pay each other fees.

