In cricket, a batter's average is the total runs scored divided by the number of times they were out. If you score 50, 0 and 100 and get out every time, your average is 150 divided by 3, which is 50.

The most famous average in sport belongs to the Australian batter Don Bradman. In 52 Test matches, he averaged 99.94. Most great batters finish their careers somewhere between about 45 and 60. [[@sachin-tendulkar|Sachin Tendulkar]], who played 200 [[@test-cricket|Test matches]], averaged a little under 54. Bradman needed just four runs in his final innings to average exactly 100. He was out for zero.

